Bernie Sanders and Chuck Schumer have  introduced a plan to restrict corporate  stock buybacks this would be an  unprecedented step in the government  basically telling business what they  could do with their money but is this  just political rhetoric or is there  something to this so the whole plan  centers around stock buybacks and that's  essentially when companies take the  money they have saved up and use it to  buy back shares in the stock market  the result is earnings per share the  profit a company makes for every share  goes up and that often drives up the  stock price the Democrats want companies  to be required to do something for their  employees give them raises improve their  pensions things like that before they  give money back to shareholders but hold  on a moment how did we get here  the political issue which you're gonna  hear about a ton over the next two years  is wages for the middle class have just  stagnated for the last 20 years the  average American family makes around  $60,000 a year and that's roughly the  same as it was 20 years ago  politicians realized there's a lot of  anger about that it's one of the reasons  Donald Trump was elected and the  Democrats are gonna try to use it to get  back into power when Donald Trump took  office soon after they had this big tax  cut and that the major impact of that  was corporate profits went up but also  the economy did really well and that  drove down the unemployment rate which  helped workers one criticism of the  Trump tax plan is that the companies  used a lot of that money and just bought  back stock and so they did not give  workers raises necessarily and what they  did do was buy back shares drive up the  stock price and as everyone knows the  people who owned stock the biggest  owners of stock are are rich people and  so this was a scene as a give back to  the rich while the workers didn't get  much the counter-argument is the money  that goes to the buybacks goes to  investors and they reinvest it and that  boosts the economy another underlying  issue is over the past couple of decades  a big trend has emerged in corporate  America so companies make revenue and  that revenue is split between profits  and wages for employees and the portion  going to profits has gone up a lot the  share taken by employees has gone down  so the idea behind the Democrats plan is  companies are going  have to give employees something a raise  better benefits etc before they can  benefit their shareholders now that goes  against tradition and you know all the  precedents before which is companies  basically can control the cash they have  one of the problems with a plan like  this is if I'm a company I'm sitting on  ten billion dollars in cash well if  there are restrictions on what I'm gonna  do in terms of a buyback I'm just not  gonna buy back the stock I'm just gonna  leave the money sitting in my bank  account and that doesn't benefit anybody  the other problem is companies are not  gonna give workers raises unless they  have to unless the wages for all other  industries around them are going up I  think the Democrats are trying to take a  stand they're trying to make this an  issue in the campaign  there are more conventional ways to  accomplish similar goals so you can do  tax cuts for working class people you  can raise the minimum wage Republican  Senator Marco Rubio has a plan out now  where they would tax essentially the  corporate buybacks it would work like a  dividend payment where the shareholders  would get a bill when the company bought  back stock so you can do tax cuts for  working class people you can raise the  minimum wage other Republicans will talk  about well we need to reduce regulation  increase investment and get the economy  growing and that's what makes workers  earn more money the Democrats just  control the House of Representatives so  this bill isn't going anywhere but the  rhetoric and the discussion around  middle-class wages and working-class  wages is going to be with us for a long  time
 

